WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.100
There are threads left dangling, prophecies
unanswered, and portentous warnings that now

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.280
seem strange and pointless.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.860
Were these questions intentionally unanswered?

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.799
Did the Game of Thrones writers simply forget
about them or purposefully shunt them to the

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.500
side when the show took unexpected turns?

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.340
From the White Walkers' introduction in the
first episode to the moment Arya Stark dispatched

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.560
them all with a well-aimed strike, they were
a terrifying mystery.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.830
Here and there, Game of Thrones dropped tantalizing
hints about the White Walkers' culture that

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.120
we never saw brought to any sort of coherent
conclusion.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:36.020
We know that the Children of the Forest created
them from human men, and that they needed

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.400
healthy human babies to continue their line.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.700
We know that they took these babies to a place
in their home in the Lands of Always Winter,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.500
which looked an awful lot like a civilized
settlement.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.700
We heard them emit what sounded like screeches,
but to them seemed to be a sort of language.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.520
Had they succeeded in tightening their frigid
grip upon the world, they might have had plans

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.900
beyond mayhem and death.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.820
Perhaps, as in the books, there were female
White Walkers hidden away up north, and a

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.880
mysterious and unique brand of ice magic unknown
to any other race.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.720
Early on in the show, Bran Stark was soothed
by the stories Old Nan told of the Children

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.660
of the Forest.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.040
They were the stuff of fantasy.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.640
Then, magic, myth, and monsters became very
real indeed.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.950
The Children, it was revealed, lived in the
far north, wielding magic in defense of the

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:27.980
Three-Eyed Raven.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:32.460
After the Walkers attacked, the Children we'd
met perished in their efforts to protect Bran

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.140
and his momentous future.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.360
Now their fate is one big question mark.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.980
Were those Children we met the last to exist?

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.860
Are there more, and can we really believe
they'll be content to keep their powerful

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.940
magic to themselves for eternity?

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.240
What does their place in the balance of power
mean for the future?

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.460
Their magic nearly brought about the end of
the world in their creation of the White Walkers,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:57.760
and they are tied to the man who is now the
king of their continent, who is one of only

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.480
two people, along with Meera Reed, to know
they exist at all.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.780
It seems safe to assume they won't stay silent
forever.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.980
In the final shot of the series, Jon Snow
marches into the woods, Ghost at this side,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:11.540
leading the Wildlings home.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.660
This is a bittersweet ending, meant as punishment
yet also as something of a spiritual reward.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.120
Jon was always at home in the true North,
unbound by Westerosi custom.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.660
He'll live on as...

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.840
"I am the sword in the darkness.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.780
I am the watcher on the walls.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.240
I am the shield that guards the realm of men."

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.540
But what does any of that mean?

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.140
The finale surrounded Jon's fate with a lot
of portentous talk but little of it presented

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.400
the audience with any concrete plans.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.340
Will they rebuild the Wall if there aren't
any White Walkers?

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.120
Will he become a new sort of King Beyond the
Wall?

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.820
Will the new Watch root out the cruelty that
flourished in isolation, like Craster's Keep

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.680
and the cannibalistic Thenns?

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.200
All we know is that Jon has returned to the
Night's Watch, just as he began the series.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.740
It's just that now, we have no clue what that
means.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.540
Religion runs wild in the world of Game of
Thrones.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.620
There's the Faith of the Seven most of Westeros
follows.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.080
There is the Many-Faced God, better known
as Death.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.819
There is the Great Stallion worshiped by the
Dothraki, who they believe will mount the

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:18.049
world in the form of a favored follower.

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:23.060
And there's the Drowned God of the Iron Islanders,
who demands devotees ritually drown.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.400
So why does the Lord of Light seem to matter
more than the rest?

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.279
From reanimating Jon Snow's dead body to lighting
the swords of thousands of Dothraki with a

00:03:31.279 --> 00:03:36.180
single chanted prayer, the followers of the
enigmatic fire god wielded actual power in

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.499
a way no other religion ever seemed to muster.

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:43.069
Some, like the Undying, were able to summon
illusions through their mystic practices,

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:46.640
while groups like the Faceless Men could assume
the forms of those they killed.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.700
But the Lord of Light proved, repeatedly,
that he could turn the tide of history with

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.260
enough sacrifice, devotion, and king's blood.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.440
Why did we only ever see miracles performed
in his name?

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.239
After seeing incredible feats performed by
his followers, will his religion gain favor

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.460
in Westeros?

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.120
Daenerys spent multiple seasons in Slaver's
Bay, and ended up mired in Meereen for three

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.100
seasons.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.120
Ruling, roasting the powerful, and chopping
the collars off the oppressed turned out to

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.120
be complicated.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.280
Word came from Yunkai and Astapor that what
she had set right had quickly gone wrong again.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.820
Ultimately, after compromise, naval warfare,
and the installment of ruling councils and

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.700
fighting pits, Daenerys left Daario Naharis
in charge when she left for Westeros, tasking

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.540
him with keeping the peace in the region she
renamed Dragon's Bay.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.940
Grey Worm and his compatriots are set to return
to Essos, but are they going to do anything

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.780
in the cities they helped conquer?

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.980
Has a leader emerged from the native culture,
as Daenerys hoped?

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.940
Did the Sons of the Harpy regroup?

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.840
The Greyjoys promised to help in exchange
for an alliance against Euron.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.800
Are any of their forces still there?

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.340
For an area that took up such a chunk of screentime,
the audience has been left with startlingly

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.340
few answers as to its fate.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.180
The sparrows were a pretty big deal for a
while.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:02.100
They were the only true, sustained threat
commoners managed to present over the course

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.220
of the series.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:09.060
Led by the High Sparrow, they were the dissatisfaction
of the public given flesh and voice, so powerful

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.130
that they brought the Tyrells to their knees
and pushed Cersei into reviving the Faith

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.140
Militant, forcing her into her walk of penance.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.820
She had to blow up the Sept of Baelor with
the High Sparrow inside just to make them

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.080
pause.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:25.400
After that explosive season finale, however,
they were never heard from again.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.760
Did they just stop?

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.600
This seems unlikely, as they were portrayed
as having a massive grassroots movement supporting

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.800
them, one that seemed to sprawl beyond the
confines of King's Landing.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.980
Their figurehead perished in the flames, but
did people really just accept the loss of

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.740
the soup kitchens the sparrows ran?

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.780
Did the Faith Militant, so fanatically devoted
they carved the seven-pointed star into their

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.900
foreheads, truly shrug and lay down their
arms?

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.940
There's a new prince of Dorne, apparently.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.600
Varys mentions him in "The Last of the Starks."

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.060
"The new prince of Dorne pledges his support."

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.760
He later showed up in Dornish yellow to vote
for Bran the Broken.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.380
That's all we know about him.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:07.240
So we're basically left to wonder: What on
earth has happened to Dorne?

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:11.980
After Oberyn's death, his daughters, the Sand
Snakes, led by his grieving lover Ellaria

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.100
Sand, led the country in rebellion.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:18.440
They murdered Oberyn's brother Doran, the
reigning Prince of Dorne, his son and heir

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.940
Trystane, and Myrcella Baratheon, both Cersei
Lannister's only daughter and Trystane's betrothed.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:29.420
This brutal coup petered out almost immediately:
Nymeria, Obara, and Tyene Sand were all killed,

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.280
and Ellaria was left to die in prison.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.520
The royal family was wiped out, then those
who wiped them out were wiped out, and then

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.200
the first prince to emerge pledged fealty
to a queen who lost.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.520
What happens in Dorne has country-wide implications,
and we've ended up having absolutely no idea

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.400
what they are.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.140
Game of Thrones characters married for prophecies,
built religions out of them, and burned others

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.180
at the stake to make them turn their way.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.800
The line between organized faith, heretical
magic, folk belief, and mainstream culture

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:58.560
was intentionally blurred.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.880
Everyone from Melisandre to Maester Aemon
gave some level of credence to the concept

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.640
of the Prince who was Promised.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.660
"Daenerys Stormborn is the one who was promised."

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.860
What isn't clear is how much any of the prophecies
mattered, if at all.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:16.100
This might have been a purposeful choice,
since Game of Thrones often subverted expectations

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.600
and fantasy cliches.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.040
But the sheer scope and story investment in
the concept of figures like Azor Ahai, and

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.020
others begs for more clarity.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.200
Did Arya fulfill them, though she wasn't,
quote, "born amid salt and smoke?"

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.980
Did Melisandre wield the power she did because
she was part of the prophecy?

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:36.040
Was Daenerys on the way to carrying the prophecy
out until she went off the rails?

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.680
Quaithe was the mysterious lady in the metal
mask who offered Daenerys a bunch of cryptic

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:41.760
advice in Qarth.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.850
We learned that she was from the shadow lands,
and she pointed Daenerys towards Pyat Pree

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:47.440
when he stole her dragons.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.420
She seemed to know things no one else did,
and was seen painting symbols on a sailor's

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.720
back to protect him from the doom surrounding
Old Valyria.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.660
She appears more often in the books, where
she pops up to impart truly opaque warnings.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.040
But in the show, she never showed up again.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.900
With her unique costume and rarefied knowledge,
not to mention her importance in the books,

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.060
she seemed to be the kind of character we'd
absolutely see again.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:14.600
But we never did, and her strange words ended
up, like the prophecies, not seeming to have

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.980
much of a place in the final few episodes.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.680
Where did her knowledge come from?

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.920
Why did she care so much about Daenerys?

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.640
Like the rest of Qarth, she receded into the
background, confined to season 2.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:31.460
One of the most interesting aspects of the
Game of Thrones world is the bizarre seasons.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:36.339
Winters and summers can last for years, a
fact elderly characters use to chide young

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.800
people ignorant of the difficulties of winter.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.160
"Oh, my sweet summer child.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.160
What do you know about fear?"

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:48.220
House Stark warns everyone that "Winter is
Coming," a signal of their inevitable strength,

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.400
but also a reminder that the summer can end
at any time.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.200
Ned Stark remarked towards the beginning of
the series that their current summer had then

00:08:55.209 --> 00:09:00.100
lasted for nine years, and its end was a big
enough deal to end season 6 with an episode

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.740
titled "The Winds of Winter."

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.740
The biggest reason for the long seasons seemed
to be the White Walkers, who "brought the

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.930
storm" as Jon Snow liked to warn, and indeed,
a sprig of grass peeking through the northern

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:14.180
snow in the series finale seemed to signify
that their defeat meant the end of winter.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:18.300
If that's so, why did the winters and summers
last so long in the centuries before their

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:19.280
return?

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.160
Was it metaphorical or literal or just bizarre
for the sake of it?

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.710
George R.R. Martin has gone on record as saying
there is a supernatural reason for the seasons

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.740
he will reveal in the books, but it didn't
make its way into the television series.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:37.040
Bran Stark began the series as a little boy
who loved to climb, and ended as Bran the

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:40.360
Broken, first of his name, King of the Andals
and the First Men.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.880
In between, he had the most conventionally
magical journey of any major character.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.340
Much was made of his ability to "warg," or
see through the eyes of people and animals,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:50.760
bending them to his will.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:56.060
Yet this power faded in favor of his ability
to perceive all events, past and present.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:59.560
Now that he's king, one wonders if warging
even mattered.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:04.020
He didn't use it to turn the tide in battle;
he used it for reconnaissance at most.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:05.860
He didn't use it to become King.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.740
It didn't much matter at all, for all the
time spent on it.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:13.600
Tyrion makes much of Bran's underdog story,
but he's also placed someone capable of mind

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:16.340
control at the top of the Westerosi hierarchy.

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:21.480
Doesn't that give anyone pause, especially
given how little Bran cares for everyday human

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.480
concerns?

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.840
"You don't want it."

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.760
"I don't really want anymore."

